## Editing a skill

A skill reaches every repo in the portfolio.

- **The owner approves the exact wording first.** Show the block; the approval is the owner's separate reply to that block. An "ok" inside a message about something else (a release, a hand-off) does not approve a text. Never delegate a skill edit to a subagent, never make one as a side effect of another task.
- **The canon is the `skill.*.md.tmpl` in the Solo Dev Hub repo.** `~/.claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` and `<repo>/docs/sdh_skills/<name>.md` are outputs, rewritten on every sync.
- **Hand-back from another repo:** edit your `docs/sdh_skills/<name>.md` first, then drop an empty `<name>.md.approved` beside it. The marker states the owner already approved this exact text — never create it on your own initiative. The next sync takes the text up and names it; check that it did. Unknown skill names are refused; two repos handing back different text for one skill are refused as a conflict.
- **Delivery does not wait for a release:** the same text through Settings → App defaults reaches every repo on the next sync. Rule changes are not `todo.md` tasks; they get one Changelog line at the next closure **of the Solo Dev Hub repo**.
- **A skill holds a dry instruction.** No case stories, project names, dates or "paid for by" — those stay in the project's retro.
- Two rules contradicting each other about one artifact is a bug in the rules: take it to the owner, don't resolve it silently.

# Agreed decisions are not reopened

A decision is **agreed** once discussed with the owner and written down (spec, task, doc, rule, or the chat that served as the spec) — including when the agent proposed it and the owner accepted. The agent does not reopen it, re-litigate it, offer alternatives "just in case", or replace it with its own measurement. Only the owner reopens it.

Every record names its author. A number or a choice the agent made alone is marked as the agent's, never written as the owner's words — and it stays open to objection. A file, field or folder that exists because of an agreed decision is its footprint, not an anomaly.

# An unproven "can't" never narrows the product

"Impossible", "the provider doesn't support it", "too expensive to check", "a code comment says so" are claims that need a source — the external system's own documentation, or a measurement. Without one, do not disable, delete, defer or trim anything, built or planned: narrowing the product is the owner's explicit decision. Say "I found no confirmation, I need the documentation" instead. When the owner refutes one such claim, re-check every claim of the same kind from the same pass.

# File formats

Escape rules for every pipe-separated format below: a literal `|` in a free-form field → `\|`, a newline → `\n`. Enum and id fields never contain `|` or newlines.

## `docs/todo.md`

`- [ ] <id> | <description> | <effort> | <priority> | <status> | <created_at>`

- `id` — required: `T-NNNNNN` (task), `F-NNNNNN` (feature), `O-NNNNNN` (technical operations), `M-NNNNNN` (marketing operations). Each prefix has its own counter: max within the prefix + 1, 6 digits. Legacy 3-digit ids still parse.
- `effort` — hours (`0` if unknown). `priority` — `critical` / `high` / `medium` / `low`. `status` — `open` / `in-progress` / `review`.
- `created_at` — `YYYY-MM-DD`, today when you add the line; never edit it afterwards.
- Release grouping: tasks sit under `## vX.Y.Z — <description>` headers (a version is `v` + digit, or a bare number with a dot or dash). Word headers (`## Операционка`) set no version.
- `O-` / `M-` are non-code work: never a release blocker, never under a version header, always empty version. Their section `## Операционка` goes first, above the version sections (nearest first).
- Done task → move it to `done.md`.

## `docs/done.md`

Under `## YYYY-MM-DD` (completion day), append at the bottom: `- <id> | <description> | <version>`. Exactly three fields; an empty id still keeps both pipes (`- | text | v1.0`). Version empty for `O-`/`M-`. No checkbox. Append-only — never edit or remove history. `D-NNNNNN` is a display-only id inside the app; elsewhere refer to the description.

## `docs/bug-reports.md`

`- <id> | <date> | <description> | <severity> | <category> | <status> | <fix_attempts> | <comment>`

- App-owned, never touch: `id` (`B-NNNNNN`), `date`, `fix_attempts`. User-owned, never touch: `description`, `severity` (`critical`/`major`/`medium`/`minor`), `category` (`ui_ux`/`ux_flow`/`logic`/`auth`/`database`/`performance`/`security`/`integration`/`other`).
- You may edit `status` and `comment`. Flow: `created` (app) → `in-progress` (you take it) → `testing` (fix applied, comment says what was done; the app counts the attempt) → `confirmed` or `rejected` (user). Rejected → back to `in-progress`.
- **Cleanup:** whenever you read the file in response to the user about bugs, delete every `confirmed` row in the same pass.
- Never create bug rows — ask the user to file it in the app, or add a `T-` task to `todo.md`.

# Solo Dev Hub auto-commits

Synced cross-repo files (REQ, API contracts, announcements) land in the working tree. If the repo has an auto-commit branch set and is on it, the app commits them itself — pathspec-scoped to the cross-repo doc folders plus `.gitignore` / `.gitattributes`, tracked paths only (a gitignored inbox is never force-added). Author `Solo Dev Hub <noreply@solodevhub.app>`, subject prefix `chore(sdh-sync):`.

Such a commit is a legitimate sync: never revert it, build on top. Synced files that are gitignored or uncommitted are equally real. If auto-commit is off and a tracked synced file blocks your work (a dirty-tree gate), commit it yourself as `chore(sdh-sync): …` without waiting to be asked.

# Versioning (SemVer)

`MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH`, each an independent counter (`0.9.x` → `0.10.0`). Start at `0.1.0`; `1.0.0` is a deliberate decision (stable, contracts frozen, in prod).

| Type | MAJOR | MINOR | PATCH |
|---|---|---|---|
| Server / microservice | breaking API change | new endpoint / parameter | fix, optimisation, refactor |
| Client | major UI overhaul, new server MAJOR | new screen / feature | visual fix, small bug |
| Tool | major change | new function | fix |

**Ask the owner before any MAJOR or MINOR bump.** PATCH for fixes is routine. Release flow: `sdh-release-lifecycle`, closure: `sdh-release-closure`. Tags, release merges and the `master`/`main` push are the owner's.

# `Changelog.md`

[Keep a Changelog](https://keepachangelog.com/): `Added` / `Changed` / `Deprecated` / `Removed` / `Fixed` / `Security`. `## [Unreleased]` has no date; at release it becomes `## [X.Y.Z] — YYYY-MM-DD`. Empty categories may be omitted in released versions.

# Commits

When the user says to commit, draft a Conventional Commit in chat before any git command:

```
<type>(<scope>): <imperative subject, lowercase, no period, ≤ 70 chars>

<what + why, 2–3 lines>

<trailers: Refs T-NNNNNN, REF: REQ-NNN, B-NNNNNN>
```

Types: `feat` `fix` `chore` `docs` `refactor` `test` `perf` `build` `ci`. Trailers are pointers to our ids, not GitHub auto-close keywords.

- Multi-branch repo (work on `dev`, `master`/`main` only gets release merges): you may commit and `git push origin dev` yourself. Single-branch repo: draft only, the user commits.
- Never push to `master`/`main`, never force-push, never push tags, never `--amend` a published commit, never skip hooks or signing.

# Shell commands

Windows PowerShell 5.1 does not understand `&&`. Give one command per line; chain with `;` if you must.

